#+startup: noindent

* emacs-tessera

Tessera provides layouts for Elfeed Search, Gnus Summary, and mu4e Headers.  You
keep using their native commands to read, search, mark, and navigate entries.

The ~tessera~ package contains all display adapters.  The ~tessera-x~ package
extends it with experimental features; ~x~ means experimental.

** Getting Started

*** Install

Use Emacs 30.1 or later and set up the mail or feed application you use.
Install ~tessera~ for display features; it requires Alert.  Install ~tessera-x~
for experiments; it depends on ~tessera~ and inherits its Alert dependency.
Experimental commands do not require enabling display modes.  They
require Gnus, mu4e 1.14.3 or later, or Elfeed 4.0.1 or later at runtime.

For a source checkout, add the core directory to ~load-path~:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/emacs-tessera/lisp/tessera/")
#+end_src

For experimental features, also add ~lisp/tessera-x/~ to ~load-path~.
The ~tessera-x-gnus~, ~tessera-x-mu4e~, and
~tessera-x-elfeed~ features are included in ~tessera-x~.  Loading their libraries
exposes options without loading the native applications.

When upgrading from separate experimental packages, replace them with
~tessera-x~ and remove their old ~load-path~ entries.  Existing feature,
command, and option names remain available.

Alternatively, create archives as described under [[*Build][Build]], then use ~M-x
package-install-file~ on the desired archives in ~dist/~.  Install ~tessera~
before ~tessera-x~.  Their archives include their Info manuals.

For Nix, add this repository as a flake input named ~tessera~ and include
~inputs.tessera.overlays.default~ in your nixpkgs overlays.  Select the packages
you want from that package set, for example:

#+begin_src nix
(pkgs.emacsPackagesFor pkgs.emacs).emacsWithPackages (
  epkgs: with epkgs; [
    tessera
    tessera-x
  ]
)
#+end_src

*** Configure

Enable the adapter for your application.  For Gnus:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-gnus)
(tessera-gnus-mode +1)
#+end_src

Loading the library makes its mode available; enabling the mode updates
existing and future matching buffers.  Pass ~-1~ to restore the native display.
Elfeed and mu4e examples appear below.

Load the feature that owns the settings you want to customize.  The view
features ~tessera-gnus-summary~, ~tessera-mu4e-headers~, and
~tessera-elfeed-search~ define their options and faces.  Experimental options
belong to the corresponding ~tessera-x-*~ feature.  Loading these libraries
does not enable a Tessera mode.

Use ~M-x customize-group~ with ~tessera~, ~tessera-elfeed~, ~tessera-gnus~, or
~tessera-mu4e~.  Installed manuals are available with ~C-h i~, then ~m~ and the manual
name, such as ~Tessera~.

** tessera

Shared display settings apply to all three adapters.  See the [[file:doc/tessera.texi][Tessera manual]] for
complete options and extension interfaces.

*** Entry Layouts and Spacing

Adapters initially use two visual lines per entry.  To select a compact layout,
evaluate this in an already enabled view:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq-local tessera-entry-layout 'single-line)
#+end_src

Use ~two-line~ to switch back.  Gnus and mu4e use their thread layouts while
native threading is enabled, regardless of this option.

Horizontal padding uses character columns; vertical padding uses normal line
heights.  For example, in your initialization file:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera)
(setopt tessera-entry-left-padding 2
        tessera-entry-right-padding 2
        tessera-entry-top-padding 0.1
        tessera-entry-bottom-padding 0.1)
#+end_src

Gnus and mu4e track shared appearance changes during their normal update cycles.
In an active Elfeed Search buffer, redraw after changing these settings with ~M-:
(elfeed-search-update :force)~.

*** Month Groups

Entry views are grouped by month by default.  Headings begin expanded; click a
heading with mouse button 1 to fold it.  Folded headings show read and unread
message or article counts, and native next and previous commands skip their
hidden entries.

Set ~tessera-month-grouping~ to nil to disable the feature globally.  Each
backend also has an ~inherit~/~t~/~nil~ override.  Gnus and mu4e threads use the
latest reply date by default; set ~tessera-month-thread-date~ to ~root~ to
group Gnus threads by the root instead.  Gnus can inherit or override that
policy.  Mu4e accepts only ~inherit~ and ~latest~; inherited ~root~ falls back
to ~latest~ because Tessera does not replace mu4e's native sorting.  While Gnus
grouping is active, Tessera uses matching buffer-local Gnus sorting and restores
the prior values and locality when grouping or the Gnus adapter is disabled.

Records without a usable date stay in native order and receive a warning
background and inline warning glyph.  See the [[file:doc/tessera.texi][Tessera manual]] for folding,
spacing, faces, date formatting, glyphs, and refresh behavior.

*** Glyphs and Faces

Glyphs use Unicode and semantic colors by default.  For monochrome ASCII
symbols:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera)
(setopt tessera-glyph-style 'ascii
        tessera-glyph-color nil)
#+end_src

Use ~unicode~ to restore Unicode symbols; ~nerd-icons~ is also supported with the
corresponding library and font.  A color string for ~tessera-glyph-color~ gives
all glyphs that foreground color.

Use ~M-x customize-face~ for ~tessera-entry-current-face~ and
~tessera-entry-hover-face~, or shared semantic faces such as
~tessera-glyph-accent-face~ and ~tessera-glyph-negative-face~.  Individual glyphs use
~:face~ to select a shared or user-defined face.

Each view has a glyph option: ~tessera-gnus-summary-glyphs~,
~tessera-mu4e-headers-glyphs~, and ~tessera-elfeed-search-glyphs~.  These are
alists of named property lists.  Supply only the fields to override; omitted
fields and glyphs retain their defaults.  For example:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-mu4e-headers)
(setopt tessera-mu4e-headers-glyphs
        '((status-unread :unicode "◉" :face tessera-glyph-accent-face)
          (attachment :hidden t)))
#+end_src

The fields are ~:ascii~, ~:unicode~, ~:nerd-icons~, ~:face~, and ~:hidden~.
Set ~:nerd-icons~ to nil to skip Nerd Icons, or ~:face~ to nil to inherit
surrounding text.  ~tessera-thread-glyphs~ configures shared connectors;
~tessera-entry-ellipsis~ configures the truncation marker.  See the manual
for glyph names, defaults, width limits, and fallback rules.

Customize and ~setopt~ refresh active views.  After ~setq~ or face changes,
use ~M-x tessera-refresh-glyphs~.  Loading a view feature makes its options
available; its mode owns runtime hooks and removes them when disabled.

*** Native Threads

Gnus and mu4e retain their native thread ordering and folding commands.  Their
~gnus-show-threads~ and ~mu4e-search-threads~ settings select the thread display.
Headings summarize the thread, while individual rows retain message state and
navigation.

Thread spacing has separate outer and inner values.  To remove gaps between
members while keeping the outer padding:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera)
(setopt tessera-thread-inner-top-padding 0
        tessera-thread-inner-bottom-padding 0)
#+end_src

*** tessera-elfeed

Enable the Search adapter:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-elfeed)
(tessera-elfeed-mode 1)
#+end_src

Rows show titles, feeds, tags, dates, and read or enclosure indicators.  Use ~M-x
customize-group~ with ~tessera-elfeed-search~ for text faces.  For example,
change the unread Unicode glyph with:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-elfeed-search)
(setopt tessera-elfeed-search-glyphs
        '((status-unread :unicode "•")))
#+end_src

Use Customize or ~setopt~ for the glyph options so their setters refresh active
Search buffers.

*** tessera-gnus

Enable ~tessera-gnus-mode~ as shown under [[*Configure][Configure]].  Summary rows show subjects,
contacts, labels, dates, and native article marks.  Attachment and security
indicators reflect information Gnus has already examined; Tessera does not
retrieve articles to populate them.

Use ~M-x customize-group~ with ~tessera-gnus-summary~ for faces such as
~tessera-gnus-summary-label-face~.  Native Gnus highlighting continues to supply
article-state styling.

*** tessera-mu4e

Enable the Headers adapter:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-mu4e)
(tessera-mu4e-mode 1)
#+end_src

Rows show subjects, contacts, labels, dates, message flags, and pending
operations.  Thread folding continues to use mu4e's own controls.

Use ~M-x customize-group~ with ~tessera-mu4e-headers~ for text faces,
including ~tessera-mu4e-headers-label-face~.  Native message-state faces remain
part of the display.

** tessera-x

A context snapshot is a read-only buffer containing selected records, metadata,
and available body text.  Load one of the experimental backend libraries below
to prepare it.  The [[file:doc/tessera-x.texi][Tessera X manual]] explains content selection and retrieval,
snapshot management, and Lisp interfaces for consumers and producers.

*** Managing Context Snapshots

Run these commands in the buffer from which you prepared the snapshot:

- ~M-x tessera-x-show-context~ displays the latest successful snapshot.
- ~M-x tessera-x-cancel-context~ cancels pending work.
- ~M-x tessera-x-discard-context~ kills the latest snapshot buffer.

A pending replacement leaves the previous successful snapshot available.  To
limit the text included in future snapshots:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x)
(setopt tessera-x-context-max-characters 100000
        tessera-x-context-body-max-characters 10000)
#+end_src

Metadata and omission notices are retained even if they exceed the total budget.
Use ~nil~ for either option to remove that limit.

*** Using Snapshot Results

~tessera-x-context-ready-hook~ receives each completed context.  This optional
user function displays its buffer when preparation finishes:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x)

(defun my-tessera-display-context (context)
  "Display the snapshot buffer of completed CONTEXT."
  (display-buffer (tessera-x-context-buffer context)))

(add-hook 'tessera-x-context-ready-hook
          #'my-tessera-display-context)
#+end_src

Other consumers, including ~gptel~ integrations, can use the same hook.  Tessera
itself does not send model requests.

*** tessera-x-elfeed

Load ~tessera-x-elfeed~ with ~require~.  The display mode is optional.  See the
[[file:doc/tessera-x.texi][Tessera X manual]].

**** Selected Entries

In Search, run ~M-x tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context~.  Native marks take
precedence over the active region; otherwise the entry at point is used.
Results are grouped by feed, preserving entry order within each feed.

**** Today's Entries

Run ~M-x tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-today-context~ in Search or Tree.  It uses the
current Search filter or Tree filter at point to select today's local entries.
It always uses stored bodies without fetching pages.

**** Linked-page Retrieval

Ordinary selected-entry snapshots fetch linked pages by default and fall back to
stored feed content on failure.  To fetch only when the stored body is short,
and limit concurrent requests:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x-elfeed)
(setopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-minimum-characters 1000
        tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-timeout 10
        tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-concurrency 2)
#+end_src

Set ~tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content~ to ~nil~ for stored content only.  These
settings do not affect today's snapshots.

*** tessera-x-gnus

Load ~tessera-x-gnus~ with ~require~ and enable Gnus Agent for the groups you use.
The display mode is optional.  See the [[file:doc/tessera-x.texi][Tessera X manual]].

**** Selected Articles

In Summary, run ~M-x tessera-x-gnus-prepare-context~.  Selection uses the active
region, then process marks, then the article at point.  Existing marks and read
state are preserved.

**** Current Subthread

Run ~M-x tessera-x-gnus-prepare-subthread-context~ for the current article and its
descendants, including folded entries.  ~C-u~ expands the selection using the
current group's local Agent overview.

To use that expanded scope by default:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x-gnus)
(setopt tessera-x-gnus-subthread-scope 'local-index)
#+end_src

**** Today's Articles

Run ~M-x tessera-x-gnus-prepare-today-context~ in Summary for the current group,
or in Group for the group or topic at point.  It reads today's locally indexed
articles and uses cached Agent bodies only.

**** Body Retrieval

Selected-article and subthread snapshots download missing bodies into the Agent
by default.  To use cached bodies only:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x-gnus)
(setopt tessera-x-gnus-body-policy 'local-only)
#+end_src

Missing bodies leave metadata and a content note in the snapshot.  Today's
snapshots always use this local-only policy.

*** tessera-x-mu4e

Load ~tessera-x-mu4e~ with ~require~.  It uses local message files and the mu index;
the display mode is optional.  See the [[file:doc/tessera-x.texi][Tessera X manual]].

**** Selected Messages

In Headers, run ~M-x tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-context~.  Marks take precedence over
the active region, followed by the message at point.  The command preserves
pending operations and read state.

**** Current Subthread

Run ~M-x tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-subthread-context~ for the current message and its
descendants, including folded rows.  ~C-u~ also searches the local mu index for
descendants outside the current results.

To expand through the index by default:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x-mu4e)
(setopt tessera-x-mu4e-subthread-scope 'local-index)
#+end_src

**** Today's Messages

Run ~M-x tessera-x-mu4e-prepare-today-context~ in Headers to use its current
query, or in Main on a bookmark or maildir query.  The result includes today's
indexed messages in that scope.  Index queries can finish asynchronously; the
ready hook runs on successful completion.

To supply a fixed maildir scope, replace the path in this example:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(require 'tessera-x-mu4e)

(defun my-tessera-today-query ()
  "Return the mail scope for today's snapshot."
  "maildir:/personal/Inbox")

(setopt tessera-x-mu4e-today-query-function
        #'my-tessera-today-query)
#+end_src

** Build

Run these commands from the checkout root.  Use Emacs, GNU Make, GNU tar, and
Texinfo.  ~nix develop~ supplies the build tools; Emacs and backend libraries must
also be available.

*** Packages

Create all package archives, or just one:

#+begin_src sh
make archive
make archive PACKAGE=tessera-x
#+end_src

Archives are written to ~dist/PACKAGE-VERSION.tar~ with the package's Info manual
and directory entry.  For descriptions, autoloads, local bytecode, and archives
together, use ~make all~.  Compilation runs Emacs with ~-Q~; provide external
library directories explicitly:

#+begin_src sh
make all EMACS_LOAD_PATH="/path/to/elfeed /path/to/mu4e"
#+end_src

The experimental package can be compiled without any external packages:

#+begin_src sh
make compile PACKAGE=tessera-x
#+end_src

~PACKAGE=...~ also selects one package for ~all~, ~compile~, and ~clean~.  ~make clean~
removes generated Lisp files and package archives.

*** Manuals

Build all Info and HTML manuals, or one package's manual:

#+begin_src sh
make -C doc all
make -C doc all PACKAGE=tessera-x
#+end_src

Use ~info~ or ~html~ in place of ~all~ for one format.  Output goes to
~dist/manuals/info/~ and ~dist/manuals/html/~.  HTML links to the GNU Emacs
stylesheet.  ~make -C doc clean~ removes these generated manuals.

** Test

The ERT runner covers both packages.  Make Elfeed and mu4e libraries
available even when you normally use only one backend.

*** Source Tests

From the checkout root, run runtime tests for both packages in separate
Emacs processes:

#+begin_src sh
make test
#+end_src

Select one package with ~make test PACKAGE=tessera~ or
~make test PACKAGE=tessera-x~.  Core tests do not load Tessera X.  Run
build and formatter regressions separately with ~make test-tools~.

The target uses ~EMACS~ (default: ~emacs~) with ~-Q --batch~.  If external
libraries are not already available, provide their directories explicitly:

#+begin_src sh
make test EMACS_LOAD_PATH="/path/to/elfeed /path/to/mu4e"
#+end_src

The runner adds the checkout's package directories to ~load-path~.  It exits with
a nonzero status if a test fails.

*** Installed Package Tests

Run the same command with ~TESSERA_TEST_PACKAGE_DIRS~ set to the colon-separated
directories of the selected packages and their Tessera dependencies.
Each must contain its ~.el~ sources
and ~.elc~ bytecode.  The runner checks that production libraries load from those
installed copies.

